The '''Office of Technology Assessment''' ('''OTA''') was an office of the [[United States Congress]] that operated from 1974 to 1995. OTA's purpose was to provide congressional members and committees with objective and authoritative analysis of the complex scientific and technical issues of the late 20th century, i.e. [[technology assessment]]. It was a leader in practicing and encouraging delivery of public services in innovative and inexpensive ways, including early involvement in the distribution of government documents through [[electronic publishing]]. Its model was widely copied around the world.

The OTA was authorized in 1972 and received its first funding in fiscal year 1974.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/bills/93/hr6691/text/enr |title=Legislative Branch Appropriation Act, 1994 |date=November 1, 1973 |publisher=U.S. Congress}}</ref> It was defunded at the end of 1995, following the 1994 mid-term elections which led to Republican control of the Senate and the House. House Republican legislators characterized the OTA as wasteful and hostile to GOP interests.

[[Princeton University]] hosts The OTA Legacy site, which holds "the complete collection of OTA publications along with additional materials that illuminate the history and impact of the agency."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.princeton.edu/~ota/ |title=Office of Technology Assessment (OTA) Legacy}}</ref> On July 23, 2008 the [[Federation of American Scientists]] launched a similar archive that includes interviews and additional documents about OTA.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://ota.fas.org/ |title=Office of Technology Assessment Archive |publisher=Federation of American Scientists}}</ref>

{{Short description|Methods for controlling the use of GMOs}}
{{Redirect|Terminator gene|DNA transcription terminators|Terminator (genetics)}}
[[File:Cotton_boll_nearly_ready_for_harvest.jpg|thumb|Plants such as an infertile cotton strain have been made in laboratories using GURT.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://discovermagazine.com/2003/aug/featgenes |title=Terminator Genes: Here's another fine mess biotechnology has gotten us into |date=1 August 2003|publisher=Discover |access-date=11 December 2018}}</ref>]]
'''Genetic use restriction technology''' ('''GURT'''), also known as '''terminator technology''' or '''suicide seeds''', is designed to restrict access to "genetic materials and their associated [[Phenotypic trait|phenotypic traits]]."<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Van Acker |first1=Rene C |last2=Szumgalski |first2=Anthony R |last3=Friesen |first3=Lyle F |date=2007-10-01 |title=The potential benefits, risks and costs of genetic use restriction technologies |url=http://www.nrcresearchpress.com/doi/10.4141/CJPS06033 |journal=Canadian Journal of Plant Science |language=en |volume=87 |issue=4 |pages=753–762 |doi=10.4141/CJPS06033 |issn=0008-4220|url-access=subscription }}</ref> The technology works by activating (or deactivating) specific genes using a controlled stimulus in order to cause second generation seeds to be either infertile or to not have one or more of the desired traits of the first generation plant.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal|last1=A.|first1=Jefferson, Richard|last2=Don|first2=Byth|last3=Carlos|first3=Correa|last4=Gerardo|first4=Otero|last5=Calvin|first5=Qualset|date=1999-04-30|title=Genetic use restriction technologies|journal=Zenodo|language=en|doi=10.5281/zenodo.1477499}}</ref><ref name="edepot.wur.nl">{{cite web |url=http://edepot.wur.nl/39033 |title=Genetic use restriction technologies (GURTs): Potential economic impacts at national and international levels |last1=Eaton |first1=D.J.F. |last2=van Tongeren |first2=Dr. F.W. |date=March 2002 |access-date=18 October 2018}}</ref> GURTs can be used by agricultural firms to enhance protection of their innovations in [[Genetically modified organism|genetically modified organisms]] by making it impossible for farmers to reproduce the desired traits on their own.<ref name="edepot.wur.nl"/> Another possible use is to prevent the escape of genes from genetically modified organisms into the surrounding environment.<ref name="GURT use" /> 

Patent applications related to a biological switch mechanism emerged in the early 1990's by companies such as [[DuPont]] and Zeneca (today [[Syngenta]]).<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Lombardo |first=Luca |date=October 2014 |title=Genetic use restriction technologies: a review |journal=Plant Biotechnology Journal |language=en |volume=12 |issue=8 |pages=995–1005 |doi=10.1111/pbi.12242 |issn=1467-7644|doi-access=free |pmid=25185773 |bibcode=2014PBioJ..12..995L }}</ref> Though the original GURT technology named "Technology Protection System" or "TPS" was developed under a cooperative research and development agreement between the [[Agricultural Research Service]] of the [[United States Department of Agriculture]] and [[Delta & Pine Land Company of Mississippi|Delta & Pine Land Company]] in the 1990s. The purpose of the development was to protect the intellectual property of biotechnology firms that the United States Department of Agriculture viewed as being a specifically American technological competence.<ref name="GURT review" /> The United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO) granted the application and issued a patent on March 3rd, 1998, the exclusive rights of the license given to the Delta & Pine Land Company through a research agreement.<ref name=":1" /> Monsanto bought Delta & Pine Land Co. acquiring its patents in 2007, although the original patent has since expired.<ref name=":1" /> The technology, while still being developed, is not yet commercially available due to the political and scientific controversies that accompanied its development.<ref name="ISF">{{cite web |url=http://www.worldseed.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/Genetic_Use_Restriction_Technologies_20030611_En1.pdf |author=www.worldseed.org, International Seed Federation|title=Genetic Use Restriction Technologies (Bangalore, June 2003)}} (Position Paper Supporting V-GURT development)</ref>

GURT was first reported on by the Subsidiary Body on Scientific, Technical and Technological Advice (SBSTTA) to the UN [[Convention on Biological Diversity]]<ref name=":0" /> and discussed during the 8th Conference of the Parties to the United Nations [[Convention on Biological Diversity]] in [[Curitiba]], Brazil, March 20–31, 2006.

